41:# Intern guide: closing the speed gap
53:# Part 1: The parts of the system that decide speed
55:## 1. What the milestone is for
68:On Candela, the 332-function path tracer that is the project's large program, the gap is of the same order. Over 100 generated cases per function, our O2 image executes 870.3 instructions per case against 747.7 for the reference compiler's `kernel` policy (IRWB-001, `various/experiments/f32-backend-01-candela-images.md`). Rendering a scene with a program specialized to that scene, ours takes 10,435 instructions per pixel on the scene B-01 and 22,851 on R-01, against 8,501 and 15,656 for `kernel` (IRWB-001 diary step 27).
70:The reference compiler does three things ours does not: it inlines calls, it rotates loops so that the loop test sits at the bottom, and it unrolls kernels. The analysis behind the Candela numbers (IRWB-001, f32 backend diary steps 8–10) found three causes on our side: calls that `kernel` inlines, f32 constants rebuilt at every use, and register copies that the allocator does not remove. This milestone addresses four items of the IRWB-002 research agenda:
79:## 2. The path from source text to executed instructions
103:## 3. The main IR: `typed.blocks`
110:- A **value** (`ValueRec`, `model.ts:166`) is a node of an expression tree: an operator, argument value ids, an optional immediate (`imm`) and an optional `type: 'f32'`. **A value has exactly one user**: trees are never shared. `get` reads a local (its `imm` is the local's index); `call` calls the function named by its `imm`.
