212:## 5. The backend in the detail the changes need
216:### 5.1 Selection
220:- **Assignments are target-directed.** `set x = e` computes `e` straight into `x`'s register (`val(s.value, localReg[s.local])`, `select.ts:747`). When `e` is a plain `get y`, the result is a register copy: `addi vx, vy, 0`, marked `mv: true` (`move`, `select.ts:162`; `val` case `get`, `select.ts:520–526`). An f32 copy is `fsgnj.s vx, vy, vy`.
221:- **Constants are rebuilt at every use.** An i32 constant that fits 12 bits and has an immediate form is folded into the instruction (`addi`, `slti`, `lw` offsets). Otherwise `li` builds it into a register: one `addi` or a `lui` and an `addi` (`select.ts:147–155`). An f32 constant is built as its bits in an x register and moved across with `fmv.w.x` (`select.ts:502–514`): two or three instructions, at every use, including uses inside loops.
224:### 5.2 Allocation
230:### 5.3 Finishing
237:### 5.4 The calling convention and frames
239:Calls follow ilp32f (`src/typed/ilp32f.ts`): integer arguments in `a0–a7`, f32 arguments in `fa0–fa7`, the rest in stack words; one result in `a0` or `fa0`; a record's leaves in `a0–a7`, `fa0–fa7` and stack words that the callee writes. A call therefore costs at least: a move per argument into its register, `jal ra`, a move of the result out of `a0`; in the callee, saving and restoring `ra` and every callee-saved register it uses, and adjusting `sp`. That fixed cost is what inlining removes, and it is a large share of a small callee's cost.
