257:## 8. Version 2 of the meaning check (IRWB-005)
261:- **More telling cases.** `strongCasesFrom` (`:329`) returns the 40 default cases unchanged, then `extra` (default 40) **boundary cases** built from pools per parameter: the boundaries of the input specification, the special values of the type (for f32: `F32_EDGES` plus thirteen patterns in `F32_EXTRA`, `:49`, among them a negative quiet NaN and a signalling NaN), and the function's own constants `c − 1, c, c + 1`. Because the default cases come first, every disagreement version 1 finds, version 2 finds too.
270:## 9. Rules as verified data (IRWB-006)
283:**Verification.** `verifyRule(rule, opts)` (`src/typed/rules/verify.ts:179`) checks a rule once, for every assignment of values to its variables that satisfies its guards, against the evaluator's semantics (i32 operators at any width in `BIN`, `src/typed/rules/semantics.ts:35`; f32 operators on bits). It applies every method that fits and records each result; a counterexample from any method rejects the rule. The recorded **strength** is that of the strongest passing method:
293:The evidence is generated once (`ttmp/2026/11/25/IRWB-006--*/scripts/04-verify-rules.mjs`, 66.6 minutes with `{ random: 1e6, seed: 7, exhaustive32: true }`) into `src/typed/rules/evidence.ts` (`RULE_EVIDENCE`, `:10`). Each record names the rule's version and the **fingerprint** of its canonical data (`ruleFingerprint`, `verify.ts:87`), and `tests/typed/rules.test.mjs` fails when a rule no longer matches its record. Seven deliberately unsound candidates (`src/typed/rules/candidates.ts`), among them `x / 2^j → x >> j` and the four float rewrites wrong over IEEE, are rejected with counterexamples; one of them, `x + (−0) → x`, is wrong only for NaNs with a non-canonical payload and passes on the meaning check's own f32 edge values (`tests/typed/rules.test.mjs:138`).
297:## 10. What the mutation measurements say (IRWB-005)
299:IRWB-005 measured the gate by **mutation testing**: it manufactured wrong programs by changing one site of an accepted revision (a constant by one, a comparison flipped or weakened, operands swapped, a shift amount changed, a bounds check dropped or weakened, a trapping statement hoisted, an f32 operator or constant changed, a store dropped; `src/typed/mutate.ts` (IRWB-005)), judged each mutant with the gate exactly as a pass is judged, and classified every mutant a gate accepted as wrong or equivalent with a cascade of exhaustive, large-sample and unconstrained searches. The **kill rate** is killed / (killed + accepted wrong); its upper bound excludes mutants presumed equivalent.
